# Full Journey — the complete customer booking lifecycle.
#
#   Login -> Explore -> Marketplace (Buy + Offplan) -> search the project -> Project -> Unit
#         -> Reserve -> Pay booking fee -> Payment method + schedule -> Sales Contract
#         -> Approve + OTP -> Signed state and price quotation persisted
#
# ACCOUNT: the NATIONAL_ID passed on the command line - there is NO default account. It must be
# supplied on every run:
#   maestro test -e NATIONAL_ID=<10-digit National ID / Iqama> .maestro/full-journey.yaml
# A run without it, or with an empty or malformed value, stops at the first step of STAGE 1,
# before the app is launched - nothing is typed, reserved or paid.
#
# --- THIS JOURNEY SPENDS MONEY -------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserving on this build is not free. "Reserve unit" leads to a booking summary whose only
# confirm control is "Pay booking fee", and the fee is charged through a real payment gateway
# before the booking exists at all. On ${PROJECT} the fee is SAR 8,855, refundable on
# completion. The card in `env` is PRE test data supplied by the team and is used for nothing
# else. Run this flow only against PRE, and only when that spend is approved.
#
# The price quotation is NOT produced at reservation time: it appears in the booking's
# Documents only AFTER the sales contract is signed, alongside a PQ expiry countdown.
#
# --- WHY THIS FLOW INSISTS ON AN OFFPLAN PROJECT -------------------------------------------
# The project type is not incidental to this case, it IS the case. A Ready Units booking was
# created end-to-end on 07/09/2026 (booking 03-01-0004-052-04-0979, Apartment 775-555) and its
# Booking Details exposed only "Documents", "Apply for online lending" and "Cancel booking" -
# no payment stage, no price quotation, no contract, and it entered neither the "Unpaid" nor
# the "Ready to sign" filter. The lifecycle simply stops there.
#
# The same account on an Offplan project reached the contract in one attempt. So this flow
# asserts the project type rather than taking whatever bookable project happens to sort first:
# selecting a Ready Units project silently produces a journey that cannot complete, and the
# failure would surface three screens later as a missing button rather than as a wrong choice.
#
# The Offplan project type is spelled three different ways by three different screens, and
# each is asserted where it is actually rendered:
#   * "Units under construction" - the project card and Project Details
#   * "Off-plan" (HYPHENATED)    - the booking summary, before the fee is paid
#   * "Offplan MOH land"         - the booking detail, after the booking exists
# The first two are checked before any money moves.
#
# --- THIS FLOW CHANGES BUSINESS DATA ON PURPOSE --------------------------------------------
# It creates a real reservation and signs a real sales contract. It is NOT idempotent: once it
# has reserved, the account holds an active booking and a re-run stops at
# "Active booking detected! You already have an active booking." Re-running needs that booking
# released through Account -> My bookings -> the booking -> Cancel booking (which requires a
# reason, and "Other reason" requires free text).
#
# --- HOW UNEXPECTED UI IS HANDLED ----------------------------------------------------------
# Overlay handling is delegated to subflows/clear-overlays.yaml rather than re-implemented.
# It takes an ANCHOR - the selector meaning "we have arrived" - clears the known first-run,
# promotional and survey surfaces, and gives the app time when neither an overlay nor the
# anchor is present. It is invoked where interstitials are known to land; elsewhere the flow
# uses targeted "runFlow when visible" guards.
#
# Nothing that writes data is ever tapped: "Update information", "Update email", the CSAT
# "Submit" and the NPS "Submit" are all specifically avoided. No error dialog is dismissed -
# an error is a result, and this flow must fail on one rather than clear it away.
#
# --- HOW TRANSITIONS ARE VERIFIED ----------------------------------------------------------
# Each stage ends by asserting content unique to its intended destination, so a flow bounced
# back to Home, Login or Explore fails there instead of somewhere misleading. Waits are on UI
# state; waitForAnimationToEnd is only ever a settle budget after a tap.
appId: fi.iwa.sakani
name: Full Journey - login to signed sales contract (Offplan)
env:
  # THE TEST USER - the single source of truth for which account this run signs in as.
  #
  # NOT A PLAIN LITERAL, deliberately. On Maestro 2.9.0 a literal here silently BEATS
  # `-e NATIONAL_ID=...` on the command line: run 2026-09-09_155536 was launched with
  # `-e NATIONAL_ID=1000011487` against a literal "1119880062", and Maestro typed 1119880062
  # into Nafath. The run looked like it targeted one account while it logged in as another.
  #
  # This expression is evaluated with the `-e` value already in scope, so the supplied value is
  # always the one used. There is deliberately NO default account: when NATIONAL_ID is not
  # supplied it resolves to "", exactly like `-e NATIONAL_ID=`, and the fail-fast check at the
  # top of STAGE 1 stops the run before the app is launched. A real default here would make a
  # run that forgot `-e` sign in - and reserve and pay - as that account (review 13/09/2026).
  #
  # Single-quoted because the expression contains ": ", which YAML reads as a mapping.
  NATIONAL_ID: '${typeof NATIONAL_ID === "undefined" ? "" : String(NATIONAL_ID)}'
  # The project this journey books. It is FOUND through Marketplace search, by name - never by
  # its position in a list. See the STAGE 2 header for why THIS project is the default.
  #   PROJECT        - matched against the search result; must identify exactly one project.
  #   PROJECT_SEARCH - what is typed into search.
  # Both are overridable, with the same self-referencing form as NATIONAL_ID and for the same
  # reason (a literal here would silently beat `-e`):
  #   maestro test -e PROJECT=<exact name> -e PROJECT_SEARCH=<term> -e MODULE=<module> .maestro/full-journey.yaml
  # Arabic defaults are kept here, in this UTF-8 file: Arabic passed through `maestro.bat -e`
  # crosses the Windows console code page.
  PROJECT: '${typeof PROJECT === "undefined" ? "مساكن طيبة المدائن EN" : String(PROJECT)}'
  PROJECT_SEARCH: '${typeof PROJECT_SEARCH === "undefined" ? "طيبة" : String(PROJECT_SEARCH)}'
  # The module (property type) to book inside that project. MODULE must match the project's
  # inventory - the default project sells Apartments.
  MODULE: '${typeof MODULE === "undefined" ? "Apartment" : String(MODULE)}'
  # Booking-fee card, PRE test data supplied by the team. It is a TEST instrument for the
  # staging payment gateway and pays the refundable SAR 8,855 booking fee, nothing else.
  CARD_NUMBER: "4464040000000007"
  # Typed as four digits: the gateway inserts the separator itself, so "12/52" would be
  # re-formatted a second time. Verified against the live form as "12 / 52".
  CARD_EXPIRY: "1252"
  CARD_CVV: "222"
  # OTP for the sales-contract signature. PRE-PROD's contract OTP is FIXED at 1234 for every
  # account (confirmed by the team). It is declared once here and referenced by name at the
  # single point it is used. Same self-referencing form as NATIONAL_ID, so
  #   maestro test -e CONTRACT_OTP=<code> .maestro/full-journey.yaml
  # is honoured - a plain literal here silently beats `-e` on Maestro 2.9.0.
  # It is ONLY the contract-signing code - it is not a login credential and is not reused
  # anywhere else in this suite.
  CONTRACT_OTP: '${typeof CONTRACT_OTP === "undefined" ? "1234" : String(CONTRACT_OTP)}'
---
# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 1 - Login and verify the booking preconditions
# ==========================================================================================

# --- FAIL FAST on the test user, before anything touches the app. The resolved value is
# written to the run log and to `output`, and must be a 10-digit Saudi National ID (1...) or
# Iqama (2...). A missing, empty or malformed NATIONAL_ID stops the run HERE, with nothing reserved or
# paid, rather than being typed into Nafath.
- evalScript: ${console.log('FULL JOURNEY TARGET NATIONAL_ID = [' + NATIONAL_ID + ']')}
- evalScript: ${output.targetNationalId = String(NATIONAL_ID)}
#
# NO "$" ANYWHERE INSIDE THIS EXPRESSION - not even a regex end-anchor. Maestro 2.9.0 silently
# skips evaluating any ${...} script that contains a second "$", and treats the raw text as a
# truthy string, so the assert PASSES whatever the value is. Verified live on this machine:
# ${/^x$/.test("abc")} and ${"a$b" === "x"} both pass, while the same checks without "$" fail.
# An earlier version of this guard used /^[12][0-9]+$/ and let "", "12345" and "abcdefghij"
# straight through to the login screen. Hence the dollar-free form: exactly 10 characters,
# no non-digit anywhere, first digit 1 (National ID) or 2 (Iqama).
- assertTrue: ${String(NATIONAL_ID).length === 10 && !/[^0-9]/.test(String(NATIONAL_ID)) && (String(NATIONAL_ID).charAt(0) === "1" || String(NATIONAL_ID).charAt(0) === "2")}

- launchApp:
    clearState: true

# The helper owns the Nafath sequence and the post-authentication interstitials, and asserts
# Home greets the account holder BY NAME - which is the "authenticated Home" check for this
# stage, since a guest Account renders a bare "Hello" with no name.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/login.yaml
    env:
      NATIONAL_ID: ${NATIONAL_ID}

# --- Eligibility, asserted from Account rather than inferred from the Reserve CTA later.
# Without this an eligibility regression would surface as a missing button on the unit screen
# and read as a unit defect rather than as an account-state problem.
- tapOn: 'Account\s*Tab 4 of 4'
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "My bookings"
    timeout: 60000

# --- WHO IS ACTUALLY SIGNED IN - read from the app, not from this flow's own variables.
# The login helper proved which ID was SUBMITTED on the Nafath screen; this proves which account
# the resulting session BELONGS TO, from the signed-in profile's own "National ID" field. It
# records output.profileNationalId only after that field matched, and the identity gate below
# requires it, so no path reaches the reservation or the payment without it.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/verify-signed-in-account.yaml
    env:
      NATIONAL_ID: ${NATIONAL_ID}

- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "(?s).*Eligibility checker status.*"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 30000
- assertVisible: '(?s).*Eligibility checker status\s*Eligible.*'

# --- IDENTITY GATE - the last check before anything that books or pays. STAGE 4 reserves and
# STAGE 5 pays a fee, so before either runs, three records must agree:
#   * output.targetNationalId  - the ID this run resolved at the top of STAGE 1;
#   * output.loginNationalId   - written by the login helper only after the Nafath screen
#                                displayed exactly that ID (what was submitted);
#   * output.profileNationalId - written by subflows/verify-signed-in-account.yaml only after
#                                the signed-in profile displayed exactly that ID (who is signed
#                                in).
# The first two are both copies of NATIONAL_ID and could never disagree on their own; the
# profile record is what makes this a real check. A missing record fails.
# Dollar-free on purpose - see the fail-fast guard at the top of this stage.
- assertTrue: ${output.loginNationalId === output.targetNationalId && output.targetNationalId === String(NATIONAL_ID) && output.profileNationalId === output.targetNationalId}

# --- NO ACCOUNT CLEAN-UP STAGE (removed 10/09/2026). Account is visited above for the two
# technical preconditions only - who is signed in, and whether that account is Eligible - and
# the journey then goes straight to Explore. It no longer opens My Bookings before the
# Marketplace and it never cancels a booking: the former STAGE 0 cancelled whatever booking the
# account already held, which is not part of the customer journey and destroyed data this run
# had not created.
#
# The account must therefore start with NO active booking. The product enforces that itself at
# "Reserve unit" ("Active booking detected!"), where this flow stops on its assertion - read the
# screen there before classifying the failure (see STAGE 3).

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 2 - Explore -> Marketplace -> search -> open the project
# ==========================================================================================

- tapOn: 'Explore\s*Tab 2 of 4'
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "Registered Interests"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Registered Interests"
    timeout: 45000

# --- MARKETPLACE. Explore's results view is the Marketplace: it is entered by choosing a
# category - Buy, with the Offplan construction status - and then lists the matching projects
# under the "Projects / Units Model" tabs (marketplace.yaml asserts the same view).
#
# --- Set the construction-status filter to Offplan.
#
# The chip opens ONE OF TWO sheets, which is the same fork marketplace.yaml, project.yaml
# and booking.yaml already handle:
#   * "Select category" - Buy/Rent plus the construction statuses, confirmed with
#     "Start searching". This is what a freshly cleared install gets, and since this flow
#     launches with clearState it is in fact the NORMAL path here. It has no "Clear"
#     control because nothing is persisted yet.
#   * "Construction Status" - the filter sheet, confirmed with "Apply", once a category
#     has been chosen before.
#
# Both are handled so the flow does not depend on leftover state. On the "Apply" variant
# "Clear" is tapped first, deliberately: that chip is multi-select and the app persists
# whatever was applied last, so selecting Offplan on top of a leftover "Ready Units"
# would return both and the journey could still land on a Ready Units project.
- tapOn: "(Construction Status|Ready Units|Readymade|Offplan|Land)"
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 8000

- runFlow:
    when:
      visible: "Start searching"
    commands:
      - tapOn: "Buy"
      - assertVisible: "Offplan"
      - tapOn: "Offplan"
      - tapOn: "Start searching"

- runFlow:
    when:
      visible: "Apply"
    commands:
      - tapOn: "Clear"
      - waitForAnimationToEnd:
          timeout: 5000
      - assertVisible: "Offplan"
      - tapOn: "Offplan"
      - tapOn: "Apply"

- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 20000

# Destination check: the Offplan filter is applied and the Marketplace results are showing.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Offplan"
    timeout: 45000
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: 'Projects\s*Tab 1 of 2'
    timeout: 45000
- takeScreenshot: journey-2-marketplace

# --- SEARCH for the project, by name.
#
# The project is found through Marketplace search, never by its position in a list: staging
# carries dozens of half-built test projects, and "the first Offplan project" lands on one of
# them - of four Offplan projects walked to a unit page on this build, two ("AF.private 1",
# "Test Private Offplan 11June PQ") expose no "Reserve unit" CTA at all. If ${PROJECT} is not in
# the current environment, search does not return it and the flow stops at the tap below, at a
# named step, instead of booking something else.
#
# WHY THIS DEFAULT. ${PROJECT} is the project on this build where the complete lifecycle -
# reserve, pay, sign, price quotation - has been executed end to end (09/09/2026, unit
# 01-01-0504-999-288, evidence/2026-09-09-full-journey-signed), with "Reserve unit" present. It
# was re-confirmed in THIS environment on 10/09/2026: Marketplace search for "${PROJECT_SEARCH}"
# lists it under "Property", and the result opens its project page
# (evidence/2026-09-10-journey-marketplace/discovery).
#
# OPENING SEARCH IS A POINT TAP, deliberately and already proven (search.yaml): the Explore
# location field exposes no text, label or id and is not itself clickable, and relative
# selectors mis-target it. 55%,11% resolves inside the field, from the Marketplace results view
# as well as from the Explore landing (verified 10/09/2026).
- repeat:
    times: 3
    commands:
      - runFlow:
          when:
            notVisible: "Popular locations"
          commands:
            - tapOn:
                point: "55%,11%"
            - waitForAnimationToEnd:
                timeout: 8000
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Popular locations"
    timeout: 30000
- tapOn: "Enter Region, City, Neighborhood, Street, Project, Developer, Building Number, Block Number"
- inputText: ${PROJECT_SEARCH}

# Results arrive grouped - Location, Property, Developer. Projects are listed under "Property";
# a "Location" row can also contain the search term (a land-plan "Project ..."), so the tap is
# constrained to the full project name AND to rows below the "Property" header.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Property"
    timeout: 30000
- takeScreenshot: journey-2-search-results
- tapOn:
    text: "(?s).*${PROJECT}.*"
    below:
      text: "Property"

# Opening a project raises the one-off "Start comparing properties" coach mark over it on a
# fresh install (seen 10/09/2026); the helper closes it with Back - never its CTA - and stops at
# the project page.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "About this project"

# --- Destination check: Project Details, and it is a bookable Offplan project.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "View units"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "About this project"
- assertVisible: "Bookings open"
- assertVisible: "(?s).*available units.*"

# The project-type assertion this whole flow exists for. "Units under construction" is STG's
# label for Offplan; a Ready Units project renders "Ready Units" here and fails this line
# immediately, which is the intended behaviour.
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Project type"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "Units under construction"

# The lifecycle preconditions that distinguish a usable project: the project must be open to
# this account, and it must actually expose a payment method.
#
# "Target audience" is NOT rendered by every project - "Murcia complex project" carries it,
# ${PROJECT} and "AF.private 1" do not - so it is checked only where the product renders it,
# and its value is then asserted. Guarding it this way keeps the check on the projects that
# have it instead of dropping it everywhere. A project restricted to another segment fails
# here rather than three screens later. Whether THIS account may book at all is not resting
# on this row in any case: STAGE 1 asserts the eligibility status from Account, and the unit
# page later has to present "Reserve unit".
- runFlow:
    when:
      visible: "Target audience"
    commands:
      - assertVisible: "For all"

# Only "Cash" is asserted under Payment methods. Every project observed on this build lists
# it; "Lending" is listed alongside it by some projects and exposed further down the Project
# details tab as "Online lending" by others, so asserting both would fail a project that is
# perfectly bookable. The check is that the project exposes a payment method at all.
- assertVisible: "Payment methods"
- assertVisible: "Cash"

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 3 - Module inside the project, then a unit inside that module
# ==========================================================================================

- tapOn: "View units"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*Unit list.*"
    timeout: 45000

# --- Module selection. "Property type" opens a sheet listing the modules this project offers.
# Its scrim removes the cards underneath from the hierarchy, so the module chip is unambiguous
# here even though the same word also labels the listings.
#
# THE CHIP IS ONLY RENDERED BY MULTI-MODULE PROJECTS. A project that sells one module offers
# no "Property type" filter at all - ${PROJECT}'s chips are Price / Area size / Eligibility
# type / Bedroom / Bathroom - so the filter is applied only when it is actually offered.
# This does NOT relax the module check: the model group tapped below carries ${MODULE} in its
# own label, so a project whose inventory is not ${MODULE} still fails, two steps down.
- runFlow:
    when:
      visible: "Property type"
    commands:
      - tapOn: "Property type"
      - extendedWaitUntil:
          visible: "Apply"
          timeout: 20000
      - assertVisible: ${MODULE}
      - tapOn: ${MODULE}
      - tapOn: "Apply"
      - extendedWaitUntil:
          visible: "(?s).*Unit list.*"
          timeout: 45000

- assertNotVisible: "Scrim"

# --- An Offplan project nests one level deeper than a Ready Units one. Its unit list shows
# MODEL GROUPS ("635 Apartments Available"), not individual units; the units live inside the
# group. That extra hop is what keeps the required Project -> Module -> Unit order honest, so
# it is walked rather than short-circuited.
# The group card is labelled "389 Apartments Available" normally, but the moment the
# "Property type" filter above has been applied the SAME card is relabelled
# "389 of 389 Apartments matched". Both spellings are accepted, because which one appears
# depends on a branch this flow deliberately takes only when the chip exists.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*(Available|matched).*"
    timeout: 45000
- tapOn: "(?s).*${MODULE}s? (Available|matched).*"

# Destination check: inside the module, listing its units.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Eligibility type"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "(?s).*Prices.*"

# --- Unit selection from within the module. Cards carry the module name plus the unit code
# as one node; index 0 makes the choice deterministic.
#
# The code format is NOT "<digits>-<digits>": it varies per project and mixes letters in
# ("Townhouse 07-05-07-05-D-E-06" on ${PROJECT}, "Apartment 5-102" elsewhere). It is matched
# loosely for that reason, but the ${MODULE} prefix is still required, so this can only ever
# select a unit of the module this journey is booking.
- tapOn:
    text: '(?s)${MODULE} [0-9A-Za-z-]+\n.*'
    index: 0

# Destination check: Unit Details for a unit of the selected module, and it is reservable.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Mortgage Calculator"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "(?s).*Unit Price.*"

# If the CTA is absent the journey stops here, on the business boundary. Read the screen
# before classifying that failure:
#   * "Active booking detected!" - the account already holds a booking. Expected on a re-run
#     until it is released. NOT a defect.
#   * "Beneficiaries only", or no message - an eligible account with no active booking is
#     expected to be able to reserve, so that is a product finding to report.
- assertVisible: "Reserve unit"
- takeScreenshot: journey-3-unit-details

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 4 - Reserve the unit
# ==========================================================================================

- tapOn: "Reserve unit"

# --- Destination check: the booking summary, with the unit carried over.
#
# THE RESERVATION SHAPE CHANGED ON THIS BUILD. An earlier revision expected
# "Disclaimer" -> "Subsidy matrix" -> "Continue" -> a separate "Booking summary" confirmed
# with "Confirm Reservation", and a "Booking timer" countdown. None of that exists any more.
# "Reserve unit" opens ONE screen, "Unit booking" / "Booking summary", which carries the
# disclaimer inline as a checkbox and commits with "Pay booking fee". Verified identically on
# two different Offplan projects, so this is the product's shape, not a per-project quirk.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Booking summary"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "Please check your reservation details below and click Confirm booking."
# The summary populates its fields progressively, so this section header can be a second or
# two behind the heading above it. assertVisible is instantaneous and read the screen too
# early; the wait keeps the same requirement without failing on render order.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Booking Details"
    timeout: 30000

# The Booking Details fields are below the fold INSIDE the scroll view. "Pay booking fee" is
# not a scroll target: it is a sticky footer button, permanently visible, so scrolling to it
# succeeds without moving the page an inch and leaves every field below the fold unread.
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Project Type"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 30000

# Second project-type gate, at the last point before the reservation is committed. This
# screen spells it "Off-plan", hyphenated, where Project Details spells the same fact
# "Units under construction"; each is matched where it is actually rendered.
- assertVisible: "Project Type"
- assertVisible: "(?s).*Off-?plan.*"

# The fee is real money, so it is asserted rather than passed over: a project whose fee or
# refund terms changed should be seen here rather than silently paid.
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Booking Fee"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 30000
- assertVisible: "Booking Fee"
- takeScreenshot: journey-4-booking-summary


# The disclaimer is a checkbox and the LABEL is not clickable - only the small box to its
# left is, and that box carries no text, id or accessibility label of its own. A `leftOf`
# selector does NOT work here: it matched a different node and popped the screen back to the
# unit list. Its bounds were read from the live hierarchy at [42,2064]-[105,2127] on this
# 1080x2400 device, giving centre (73,2095) = 7%,87%. The box sits in the sticky footer just
# above "Pay booking fee", so it does not move as the summary scrolls.
#
# The point is ABSOLUTE, not a percentage. "7%,87%" was tried and lands SHORT of the box:
# 87% of the 2400px screen is y=2088, inside the box, yet the tap missed every time -
# Maestro appears to resolve percentages against a smaller reference height than the full
# screen. Absolute pixels hit it reliably (verified by toggling the CTA from disabled to
# enabled). This ties the step to the 1080x2400 emulator, which this suite targets anyway.
#
# The page must be SETTLED before this tap - tapping while the scroll above is still
# animating misses the box, and the miss is SILENT: the tap reports success and the checkbox
# stays clear.
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 8000

# GUARDED, because the acceptance is REMEMBERED. On an account that has reserved before, the
# app renders this screen with the box already ticked and the CTA already enabled - and an
# unconditional tap would then UNCHECK it and disable the button, failing the run on a
# screen that was ready to proceed. The condition reads the CTA's enabled state, which is
# the only observable difference: the checkbox node itself carries no checked attribute.
- runFlow:
    when:
      notVisible:
        text: "Pay booking fee"
        enabled: true
    commands:
      - tapOn:
          point: "73,2095"
      - waitForAnimationToEnd:
          timeout: 5000

# The CTA is disabled until the disclaimer is accepted, and Maestro taps a disabled node
# happily - so the miss is not caught here. It surfaces at the "Payment Gate" wait below,
# which is still before any money moves.
- tapOn: "Pay booking fee"

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 5 - Pay the booking fee at the payment gateway
# ==========================================================================================
#
# "Pay booking fee" opens the NHC "Immediate Payment" gateway in a WebView. This is a REAL
# payment of a real fee (SAR 8,855 on ${PROJECT}, refundable on completion) using the PRE
# test card supplied by the team. Nothing else in this flow spends money.
#
# The gateway renders in Arabic by default; it is switched to English first so every selector
# below is stable text rather than a coordinate.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Payment Gate"
    timeout: 60000
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*(Immediate Payment|الدفع اللحظي).*"
    timeout: 90000
- runFlow:
    when:
      visible: "English"
    commands:
      - tapOn: "English"
      - waitForAnimationToEnd:
          timeout: 8000

# The amount is asserted before it is paid. A fee that changed is a thing to see, not to pay.
- assertVisible: "Wanted Amount To Be Paid"
- assertVisible: "Payment Methods"

# Mada, not Visa: the supplied test card's BIN (446404) is a mada BIN.
- tapOn: "Pay With Mada"

# The form paints its fields while a spinner is still over them, and typing into it during
# that window fails SILENTLY: focus never leaves the first field, so the expiry and holder
# text is appended to the card number instead ('4464 0400 0000 0007 912'). Taps, TAB and
# Escape all fail to move focus until it has settled. Waiting for the field labels to be
# individually exposed, and then giving the form a settle budget, is what makes this stage
# reproducible rather than a coin toss.
# The asterisk spacing on these labels is NOT stable between renders: the same gateway has
# produced both "Card Holder*" and "Card Holder *" on this build, and a selector written for
# one form fails against the other while the field sits plainly on screen. Each label is
# therefore matched with an optional space and a LITERAL asterisk - `\*`, escaped, because
# these selectors are regexes and a bare `*` is a quantifier that would match neither form.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: 'Card Number ?\*'
    timeout: 90000
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: 'Card Holder ?\*'
    timeout: 60000
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 10000

# Each field is addressed by the label above it. Card number and expiry are erased first
# because the gateway keeps whatever a previous attempt left in them, and it silently
# APPENDS otherwise - an earlier attempt produced "4464 0400 0000 0007 912" that way.
- tapOn:
    below:
      text: 'Card Number ?\*'
- eraseText:
    charactersToErase: 30
- inputText: ${CARD_NUMBER}
- hideKeyboard
- tapOn:
    below:
      text: 'Expiry Date ?\*'
- eraseText:
    charactersToErase: 10
- inputText: ${CARD_EXPIRY}
- hideKeyboard
- tapOn:
    below:
      text: 'Card Holder ?\*'
# Card Holder is filled with the CVV test value (CARD_CVV) - decided 10/09/2026, because no
# holder name was supplied with the team's test card. This step used to type a CARD_HOLDER
# variable that was never defined anywhere, so the literal text "undefined" went into the
# gateway (run 2026-09-09_151439, maestro.log:1889). Cleared first, like Card Number and
# Expiry Date above, so nothing pre-filled or half-typed can be appended to.
- eraseText:
    charactersToErase: 30
- inputText: ${CARD_CVV}
- hideKeyboard

# CVV is below the fold. visibilityPercentage is lowered because the label sits close to the
# bottom edge of the WebView and is never 100% clear of it.
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: 'CVV ?\*'
    direction: DOWN
    visibilityPercentage: 60
    timeout: 30000
- tapOn:
    below:
      text: 'CVV ?\*'
- inputText: ${CARD_CVV}
- hideKeyboard

- assertVisible: "Pay now"
- tapOn: "Pay now"

# --- DEFECT: the app EXITS TO THE ANDROID LAUNCHER after a successful payment. There is no
# success screen, no return to the booking and no error - the process simply leaves the
# foreground. The payment itself succeeds (verified: the booking reads Active/Paid
# immediately afterwards), so this is a navigation defect, not a payment failure.
#
# The flow therefore does NOT wait for an in-app confirmation here, because none is shown.
# It relaunches and reads the result from the product's own records instead, which is the
# stronger check anyway.
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 30000
- launchApp

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 6 - The booking exists, is paid, and is awaiting signature
# ==========================================================================================

# First the Home the relaunch lands on.
#
# LEAVE HOME AT ONCE (13/09/2026). After this relaunch the app lands on Home, and on Home the NPS
# survey re-opens BY ITSELF about 10 s after it is closed - shown with screenshots only and no
# automation input (closed 13:03:52, back 13:04:02; evidence/2026-09-13-nps-close). A helper that
# waits on Home for the survey to STAY closed can therefore never hand back (run 2026-09-13_123054
# failed exactly so). The survey does not follow onto Account (60 s observed), so the answer is to
# leave Home the moment it is closed:
#   1. the shared helper clears everything else (the CSAT lands first here - its "Dismiss", never
#      Submit) and stops as soon as the Account tab OR the survey is up. Its exit contract is waived
#      (anchorOptional) because on Home the survey is EXPECTED back; its final sweep still closes a
#      visible survey and still withholds every gesture while a product error is on screen;
#   2. if the survey is up now, its close X - 73,1434, guarded exactly as in clear-overlays: survey
#      AND Submit visible, no product error;
#   3. the Account tab at once. Optional, because if the survey came back first the tab is not in
#      the hierarchy (the sheet removes it), so nothing is ever tapped blind.
# At most 5 rounds, ending the moment "My bookings" (Account) is on screen. Submit and the ratings
# are never tapped. A product error is never cleared here: the next helper call fails on it.
- repeat:
    times: 5
    while:
      notVisible: "My bookings"
    commands:
      - evalScript: ${output.anchorOptional = true}
      - runFlow:
          file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
          env:
            ANCHOR: '(?s)(Account\s*Tab 4 of 4|.*would you recommend benefiting from Sakani.*)'
      - evalScript: ${output.anchorOptional = false}
      - runFlow:
          when:
            visible: "(?s).*would you recommend benefiting from Sakani.*"
          commands:
            - runFlow:
                when:
                  notVisible: '(?s)(.*There.s something wrong.*|.*the service is down.*|Error!)'
                commands:
                  - runFlow:
                      when:
                        visible: "Submit"
                      commands:
                        - evalScript: "${console.log('LEAVE HOME (after payment) - NPS up, closing it with its X, then Account at once')}"
                        - tapOn:
                            point: "73,1434"
                            retryTapIfNoChange: false
      - tapOn:
          text: 'Account\s*Tab 4 of 4'
          optional: true
# Once the account has reserved, the NPS survey re-arms on navigation. Observed live on
# PRE-4.7.6-1283: this exact tab tap succeeded, the survey came straight back over Account,
# and "My bookings" could not be found. The helper clears it with its header-strip drag -
# never Submit, never a rating - and then proves Account is actually reachable.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "My bookings"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "My bookings"
    timeout: 60000
- tapOn: "My bookings"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "My Bookings"
    timeout: 45000

# Destination check: the reservation was accepted. This is asserted on the booking record
# rather than on a success toast - the toast does not exist on this build (see above).
- tapOn: 'Active\s*Tab 2 of 4'
# The payment status is a line of its own on the card ("<project>\nActive\nPaid\nUnit Type...",
# read from the device 10/09/2026). It is matched as that whole line: ".*Paid.*" also matched
# "Unpaid", because selectors are case-insensitive (review 13/09/2026).
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*\nActive\nPaid\n.*Unit code.*"
    timeout: 60000
- assertNotVisible: "(?s).*don.t have any active booking.*"

# The booking is in the signable set BEFORE it is signed. STAGE 8 asserts it has left again,
# and the pair is what proves the signature took effect rather than merely being accepted.
- tapOn: "Ready to sign"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*\nActive\n.*Unit code.*"
    timeout: 45000

- tapOn: "All"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*\nActive\n.*Unit code.*"
    timeout: 30000
- tapOn: "(?s).*\nActive\n.*Unit code.*"

# Destination check: the Offplan booking detail. "Offplan MOH land" is the third and final
# project-type gate, and the fee is confirmed settled on the product's own record.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Booking Details"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "Offplan MOH land"
- assertVisible: "Booking fee status"
- assertVisible: "Paid"
- assertVisible: "Invoice number"
- takeScreenshot: journey-6-booking-paid

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 7 - Payment method, then the sales contract
# ==========================================================================================
#
# "Select payment method" opens "Finalize sales contract", Step 1 of 2. BOTH a method and a
# schedule have to be chosen: selecting the method alone leaves "Save and continue" inert,
# which reads as a dead button rather than as a missing choice.
#
# Cash is chosen over Lending deliberately. Lending hands off to an external bank application
# that cannot complete inside a test run; Cash is the path that reaches the contract.
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Select payment method"
    direction: UP
    timeout: 30000
- tapOn: "Select payment method"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Finalize sales contract"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "Step 1 of 2"

- tapOn: "(?s)Cash\nYou will pay the unit price Cash.*"

# The CSAT survey ("Overall, how satisfied are you with the ease of use of the Sakani
# platform?") lands ON TOP of this step, between choosing the method and the schedule
# appearing. It is cleared here rather than waited out - and cleared with its "Dismiss",
# never its "Submit", which would post a real survey response.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "Select payment schedule"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Select payment schedule"
    timeout: 45000
- tapOn: "(?s).*payments\nUnit Price.*"
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 8000
- tapOn: "Save and continue"

# --- Destination check: the draft contract with both actions presented.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Sign sales contract"
    timeout: 60000
- assertVisible: "Step 2 of 2"
- assertVisible: "Approve"
- assertVisible: "Reject"

# "Reject" is never tapped anywhere in this flow - it cancels the booking.
- tapOn: "Approve"

# --- Verification code. Approve raises a 4-digit OTP screen; the code is sent by SMS to the
# account's registered mobile and expires in roughly two minutes, so nothing slow belongs
# between the tap above and the entry below.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "Verification Code"
    timeout: 45000
- assertVisible: "(?s).*Code has been sent to.*mobile number.*"
- assertVisible: "OTP"

# The four digit boxes each carry the accessibility label "X" (the empty-box placeholder) and sit
# between the "OTP" label and the "OTP Expires in:" timer. The screen submits on its own once the
# fourth digit lands - there is no Submit button - so the code is typed once, after focusing the
# FIRST box, and focus advances automatically as digits arrive.
#
# The tap is constrained to that band on purpose. The numeric keyboard is already up when this
# screen opens, and a bare `below: "OTP"` resolved to the keyboard's "1" key (run
# 2026-09-10_170119: resource-id com.google.android.inputmethod.latin:id/key_pos_number_line1_1,
# tap at 138,1731). That typed an extra "1" before the code, so the app received a wrong
# four-digit value and answered "Otp is invalid or expired". `above: "OTP Expires in:"` keeps the
# tap off the keyboard (and off its clipboard suggestion chips) entirely.
- tapOn:
    text: "X"
    below:
      text: "OTP"
    above:
      text: "OTP Expires in:"
    index: 0
- inputText: ${CONTRACT_OTP}
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 15000

# ==========================================================================================
# STAGE 8 - Final validation, on the product's own records
# ==========================================================================================
#
# An accepted OTP is NOT the pass condition. What has to be true is that the contract is
# actually signed and the booking has left the signable state, so the checks below re-read
# the booking from a fresh launch rather than trusting the screen the OTP left behind.

- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*Sales contract has been signed.*"
    timeout: 90000
- assertNotVisible: "(?s).*(Invalid|incorrect|expired).*"
- assertNotVisible: "There's something wrong. Please try again later!"

- launchApp
# After the signing the survey re-arms hardest - same block as after the post-payment relaunch.
#
# LEAVE HOME AT ONCE (13/09/2026). After this relaunch the app lands on Home, and on Home the NPS
# survey re-opens BY ITSELF about 10 s after it is closed - shown with screenshots only and no
# automation input (closed 13:03:52, back 13:04:02; evidence/2026-09-13-nps-close). A helper that
# waits on Home for the survey to STAY closed can therefore never hand back (run 2026-09-13_123054
# failed exactly so). The survey does not follow onto Account (60 s observed), so the answer is to
# leave Home the moment it is closed:
#   1. the shared helper clears everything else (the CSAT lands first here - its "Dismiss", never
#      Submit) and stops as soon as the Account tab OR the survey is up. Its exit contract is waived
#      (anchorOptional) because on Home the survey is EXPECTED back; its final sweep still closes a
#      visible survey and still withholds every gesture while a product error is on screen;
#   2. if the survey is up now, its close X - 73,1434, guarded exactly as in clear-overlays: survey
#      AND Submit visible, no product error;
#   3. the Account tab at once. Optional, because if the survey came back first the tab is not in
#      the hierarchy (the sheet removes it), so nothing is ever tapped blind.
# At most 5 rounds, ending the moment "My bookings" (Account) is on screen. Submit and the ratings
# are never tapped. A product error is never cleared here: the next helper call fails on it.
- repeat:
    times: 5
    while:
      notVisible: "My bookings"
    commands:
      - evalScript: ${output.anchorOptional = true}
      - runFlow:
          file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
          env:
            ANCHOR: '(?s)(Account\s*Tab 4 of 4|.*would you recommend benefiting from Sakani.*)'
      - evalScript: ${output.anchorOptional = false}
      - runFlow:
          when:
            visible: "(?s).*would you recommend benefiting from Sakani.*"
          commands:
            - runFlow:
                when:
                  notVisible: '(?s)(.*There.s something wrong.*|.*the service is down.*|Error!)'
                commands:
                  - runFlow:
                      when:
                        visible: "Submit"
                      commands:
                        - evalScript: "${console.log('LEAVE HOME (STAGE 8) - NPS up, closing it with its X, then Account at once')}"
                        - tapOn:
                            point: "73,1434"
                            retryTapIfNoChange: false
      - tapOn:
          text: 'Account\s*Tab 4 of 4'
          optional: true
# Once the account has reserved, the NPS survey re-arms on navigation. Observed live on
# PRE-4.7.6-1283: this exact tab tap succeeded, the survey came straight back over Account,
# and "My bookings" could not be found. The helper clears it with its header-strip drag -
# never Submit, never a rating - and then proves Account is actually reachable.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "My bookings"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "My bookings"
    timeout: 60000
- tapOn: "My bookings"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "My Bookings"
    timeout: 45000

# The load-bearing assertion of the whole journey: the booking must no longer be awaiting
# signature. Before signing this filter listed it (STAGE 6); if it still does, the OTP screen
# closed without the signature taking effect.
#
# The NPS survey lands hard right after signing and RE-APPEARS on navigation, so the surfaces
# are cleared against this screen rather than assumed away.
- runFlow:
    file: subflows/clear-overlays.yaml
    env:
      ANCHOR: "Ready to sign"
- tapOn: "Ready to sign"
- waitForAnimationToEnd:
    timeout: 12000
- assertNotVisible: "(?s).*\nActive\n.*Unit code.*"
- assertVisible: "(?s).*don.t have any booking.*"

# And the booking itself still exists and carries its signed state.
- tapOn: "All"
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*Unit code.*"
    timeout: 45000
- tapOn: "(?s).*\nActive\n.*Unit code.*"

# The booking detail paints its steps card first: wait for it, then SCROLL to the documents.
# Once the booking is signed, "Documents" sits below the fold - the steps card and the Booking
# Details section fill the screen - and rows below the fold are not in the hierarchy at all, so
# a plain wait for "Documents" can never succeed. Review 13/09/2026, run 2026-09-13_093951: it
# failed here after 45s with "Documents" absent from the hierarchy while this very booking -
# signed ("Sign contract" dated 10/09/2026) and paid - was on screen.
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible: "(?s).*steps? left.*"
    timeout: 45000
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Sales contract"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 30000

# The signed contract is retrievable and recorded as signed, the price quotation has been
# issued, and the app no longer asks for a signature. "Signed" is full-match, so the draft's
# "Unsigned" cannot satisfy it.
- assertVisible: "Documents"
- assertVisible: "Sales contract"
- assertVisible: "Signed"
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Price quotation"
    direction: DOWN
    timeout: 30000
- assertVisible: "Price quotation"
- assertNotVisible: "(?s)Sign sales contract\nYour booking will not be complete.*"
# The signed booking still records the fee as settled, with its invoice. On this screen these
# sit in the Booking Details section ABOVE Documents (09/09 dump final-detail.xml: Invoice
# number / Booking fee status / Paid are one scroll above Documents), hence the scroll back UP.
- takeScreenshot: journey-8-documents-signed
- scrollUntilVisible:
    element:
      text: "Invoice number"
    direction: UP
    timeout: 30000
- assertVisible: "Booking fee status"
- assertVisible: "Paid"
- assertVisible: "Invoice number"
- takeScreenshot: journey-8-booking-paid-and-signed
